/* Content transcribed from Rafeeq_Final_Updated_Proposal v3.docx. Regenerate with tools/export_proposal.py. */
Rafeeq.proposal = {"navigation": "<div class=\"proposal-nav-inner\"><div class=\"topic-links\"><button class=\"topic-link\" type=\"button\" data-topic=\"proposal-1\" aria-controls=\"proposal-1\">The idea</button><button class=\"topic-link\" type=\"button\" data-topic=\"proposal-2\" aria-controls=\"proposal-2\">The problem</button><button class=\"topic-link\" type=\"button\" data-topic=\"proposal-3\" aria-controls=\"proposal-3\">Use cases</button><button class=\"topic-link\" type=\"button\" data-topic=\"proposal-4\" aria-controls=\"proposal-4\">Assisting Tools metrics</button><button class=\"topic-link\" type=\"button\" data-topic=\"proposal-5\" aria-controls=\"proposal-5\">Project summary</button><button class=\"topic-link\" type=\"button\" data-topic=\"proposal-6\" aria-controls=\"proposal-6\">Our solution</button><button class=\"topic-link\" type=\"button\" data-topic=\"proposal-7\" aria-controls=\"proposal-7\">What Rafeeq gives</button><button class=\"topic-link\" type=\"button\" data-topic=\"proposal-8\" aria-controls=\"proposal-8\">The 18 ADHD symptoms: what we can and cannot measure</button><button class=\"topic-link\" type=\"button\" data-topic=\"proposal-9\" aria-controls=\"proposal-9\">Features</button><button class=\"topic-link\" type=\"button\" data-topic=\"proposal-10\" aria-controls=\"proposal-10\">Shared functional capabilities</button><button class=\"topic-link\" type=\"button\" data-topic=\"proposal-11\" aria-controls=\"proposal-11\">Additional features</button><button class=\"topic-link\" type=\"button\" data-topic=\"proposal-12\" aria-controls=\"proposal-12\">General guidelines</button><button class=\"topic-link\" type=\"button\" data-topic=\"proposal-13\" aria-controls=\"proposal-13\">Functional and non-functional requirements</button><button class=\"topic-link\" type=\"button\" data-topic=\"proposal-14\" aria-controls=\"proposal-14\">Recommended features (future work)</button><button class=\"topic-link\" type=\"button\" data-topic=\"proposal-15\" aria-controls=\"proposal-15\">Competitive landscape</button></div><details class=\"topic-more\"><summary>More<span class=\"topic-chevron\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span></summary><div class=\"topic-menu\"></div></details></div>", "content": "<section class=\"panel proposal-section\" id=\"proposal-1\" aria-labelledby=\"heading-1\"><h2 id=\"heading-1\">What is Rafeeq?</h2><p><strong>Rafeeq</strong> (Arabic for “companion”) is an AI support system for children with ADHD, built on one idea: every child’s ADHD looks different, so the support should be shaped around the child instead of the other way around.</p><ul><li><strong>Throughout the day</strong>, one companion character, personalised through rewards and badges, stays with the child wherever he is. It notices early signs of overwhelm, offers the Calm Down Menu and Focus Mode whenever he needs them, learns his best focus and sleep times, and sends him only the gentle reminders he needs for what is happening now and next.</li><li><strong>At home</strong>, the companion explains and recaps lessons and tests him in whatever way suits him (tapping, speaking, typing, writing or reading aloud). It checks his homework from a photo, notices when he drifts off task, and helps with the homework he or his parent uploads and with chores.</li><li><strong>At school</strong>, Rafeeq coaches the teacher on how to respond when the child needs a rest, loses focus or gets overstimulated. It notes when attention drops in a lesson, so the missed content can be covered at home, and it reminds him to check his belongings before leaving school.</li><li><strong>Around the child</strong>, the child, parent, teacher and doctor each see different information, in short and clear summaries.</li><li><strong>Underneath</strong>, one AI system shares one memory and one prediction engine, so every part of Rafeeq learns from the same picture of the child. The companion is an AI agent: it remembers the child and can act for him, not only chat.</li></ul><p>Rafeeq is a coaching and support tool. It is not a medical device, it does not diagnose, and it does not replace professional care.</p></section><section class=\"panel proposal-section\" id=\"proposal-2\" aria-labelledby=\"heading-2\" hidden><h2 id=\"heading-2\">The problem</h2><p>Most ADHD tools are built around one slice of a child’s day, and the adults around the child rarely see the same picture.</p><p><strong>7 million</strong></p><p>US children aged 3–17 (11.7%) have a current ADHD diagnosis. CDC, 2024 parent survey.</p><p>Support for a child with ADHD is split into pieces that never meet, and most of it is not built around how the child behaves. Six gaps stand out:</p><ul><li><strong>Every tool solves one slice.</strong> A wearable vibrates, a game trains attention, a planner lists tasks, a clinic portal stores notes. None of them share what they learn.</li><li><strong>The adults around a child see different pictures.</strong> Parents see homework fights, a teacher sees a distracted student, a doctor sees fifteen minutes every few months. Nobody sees the whole week.</li><li><strong>Most tools react late.</strong> Alerts fire after the missed deadline or the meltdown. Early signs (rising heart rate, restlessness, a skipped break) go unnoticed.</li><li><strong>Screen time is measured by the clock, not the child.</strong> An hour of quiet drawing and an hour of flashing videos count the same.</li><li><strong>Signs are spotted late.</strong> Many parents sense something is different but have no simple way to know what to look for.</li><li><strong>Every child is stimulated differently.</strong> A bright, busy character that works for one child can overwhelm another.</li></ul><h3>What this looks like day to day</h3><div class=\"proposal-table table-scroll\" role=\"region\" tabindex=\"0\" aria-label=\"What this looks like day to day — scrollable table\"><table><caption>What this looks like day to day</caption><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\"><p><strong>AT SCHOOL</strong></p></th><th scope=\"col\"><p><strong>AT HOME</strong></p></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p>Attention that comes and goes unpredictably</p><p>Missing part of a lesson without anyone noticing</p><p>Overstimulation that builds before anyone sees it</p><p>Teachers unsure when a child or the class needs a rest</p><p>Early signs that go unnoticed in a busy classroom</p></th><td><p>Refusing tasks that sound like orders</p><p>Homework fights over what was missed in class</p><p>Drifting off during homework, and the same mistakes repeated without anyone noticing</p><p>Forgotten items and broken routines</p><p>Fast, flashing videos that wind the child up</p><p>A gap between home, school and the doctor’s office</p></td></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"panel proposal-section\" id=\"proposal-3\" aria-labelledby=\"heading-3\" hidden><h2 id=\"heading-3\">Use cases</h2><p>Rafeeq is divided into <strong>five use cases</strong>, one for each place the app is used and each person who uses it. The child has three: one for the support that follows him throughout the day, one for home and one for school. The parent and the psychiatrist have one each.</p><div class=\"proposal-table table-scroll\" role=\"region\" tabindex=\"0\" aria-label=\"Use cases — scrollable table\"><table><caption>Use cases</caption><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\"><p><strong>#</strong></p></th><th scope=\"col\"><p><strong>USE CASE</strong></p></th><th scope=\"col\"><p><strong>WHO</strong></p></th><th scope=\"col\"><p><strong>WHERE</strong></p></th><th scope=\"col\"><p><strong>WHAT RAFEEQ DOES</strong></p></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>UC1</strong></p></th><td><p>Child throughout the day</p></td><td><p>Child</p></td><td><p>Anywhere: home, school and in between</p></td><td><p>Features that <strong>follow the child across every setting</strong>: the personalised agentic companion, early overwhelm detection, personalised rewards and badges, focus and sleep prediction, the Calm Down Menu, Focus Mode, and gentle reminders for what is happening now and next.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>UC2</strong></p></th><td><p>Child at home</p></td><td><p>Child</p></td><td><p>Home, evenings, weekends</p></td><td><p>Lesson explanation and recap with multisensory tests and reading-aloud checks, the AI homework assistant (homework uploaded by the child or parent and broken into small steps), homework photo checking, off-task nudges during homework, and gamified chores.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>UC3</strong></p></th><td><p>Child at school</p></td><td><p>Child, with the teacher (class or shadow teacher) receiving guidance</p></td><td><p>Classroom</p></td><td><p>A dedicated camera and the smartwatch detect focus, fidgeting and movement. Rafeeq tells the teacher <strong>how to deal with the child</strong>, not only that he is unfocused, and reminds the child about his belongings before leaving.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>UC4</strong></p></th><td><p>Parent</p></td><td><p>Parent or caregiver</p></td><td><p>Overall life</p></td><td><p>Builds the routine and chores, receives the complete day plan, verifies medication, and gives weekly feedback for symptoms the app cannot measure.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>UC5</strong></p></th><td><p>Psychiatrist</p></td><td><p>Psychiatrist or clinician</p></td><td><p>Clinic, between appointments</p></td><td><p>Receives a weekly report: medication, the 18 ADHD symptoms over time, and patterns.</p></td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>UC1 · Child throughout the day</h3><p>These features are not tied to one place. They go with the child at home, at school and in between, as far as the available devices and the parent’s consent allow.</p><ul><li><strong>Personalized agentic companion:</strong> one consistent 3D animated character that adapts its voice, tone, pace and style to him, remembers his interests and what helped before, and can act for him (start a timer, add a task, open a lesson) within the parent’s settings.</li><li><strong>Early overwhelm detection:</strong> watch signals (heart rate, HRV, movement, sleep), with opt-in voice and camera cues, catch rising overload early. It works wherever he is, depending on which devices are available and what the parent has consented to.</li><li><strong>Personalized rewards and badges:</strong> one reward system shared by homework, chores, routines, lessons and other activities, with badges for his own progress and customisation items for his companion (clothes, hats, accessories, pets).</li><li><strong>Personalized focus and sleep prediction:</strong> sleep metrics from the watch show how long he sleeps. From his history, Rafeeq finds his <strong>best focus time</strong>, plans study around it and predicts focus dips before they happen.</li><li><strong>Calm Down Menu:</strong> he can open it himself at any time to name a feeling and pick a calming activity. The companion also offers it when early overwhelm detection sees signs rising.</li><li><strong>Focus Mode:</strong> silences distractions and keeps him in the current task whenever he needs to concentrate, at home, at school or anywhere else.</li><li><strong>Personalized notifications and reminders:</strong> he sees what he is doing <strong>right now</strong> and what is next, with gentle transition reminders. The complete day plan goes to the parent (UC4).</li></ul><h3>UC2 · Child at home</h3><p>There are <strong>two types of revision</strong>, each with its own testing.</p><div class=\"proposal-table table-scroll\" role=\"region\" tabindex=\"0\" aria-label=\"UC2 · Child at home — scrollable table\"><table><caption>UC2 · Child at home</caption><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\"></th><th scope=\"col\"><p><strong>LESSON EXPLANATION</strong></p></th><th scope=\"col\"><p><strong>LESSON RECAP</strong></p></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>When</strong></p></th><td><p>New lesson, or one he missed</p></td><td><p>Revising a lesson he already had</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>Content</strong></p></th><td><p>Full explanation by his companion, step by step</p></td><td><p>Only the <strong>main points</strong> (summary)</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>During</strong></p></th><td><p>Small quizzes throughout</p></td><td><p>Short, quick checks</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>After</strong></p></th><td><p>Comprehension test to confirm he understood</p></td><td><p>Test on the main points</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>How he answers</strong></p></th><td><p>Multisensory: choose (MCQ), say it aloud, type it, write it by hand, drag and build, or read aloud</p></td><td><p>The same answer options as the explanation</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>Feedback</strong></p></th><td><p>Feedback on weak spots, then reward</p></td><td><p>Feedback, then reward</p></td></tr></tbody></table></div><ul><li><strong>Five senses:</strong> each explanation uses several senses: sight (visuals, animation), sound (speech, music), touch (tap, drag, build), movement (act it out) and, where possible, real objects at home.</li><li><strong>Different formats:</strong> the same lesson can be text, speech, video or visuals, depending on how he likes to learn.</li><li><strong>Multisensory tests:</strong> every quiz and test, during and after the explanation and the recap, lets him answer in the way that suits him. He can choose an answer (MCQ), say it aloud, type it, write it by hand, or drag and build it. Spoken and written answers are graded by meaning, not by exact wording, so he is not marked wrong for saying the right idea in his own words.</li><li><strong>Reading skills in Arabic and English:</strong> some test questions ask him to read a sentence or a short paragraph aloud. Rafeeq catches skipped and misread words, measures his reading pace, and gives encouraging feedback. This is part of the lesson tests, not a separate feature, and his reading progress is tracked over the weeks.</li></ul><h3>Other things the child gets at home</h3><div class=\"proposal-table table-scroll\" role=\"region\" tabindex=\"0\" aria-label=\"Other things the child gets at home — scrollable table\"><table><caption>Other things the child gets at home</caption><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\"><p><strong>NEED</strong></p></th><th scope=\"col\"><p><strong>WHAT RAFEEQ DOES</strong></p></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>Homework</strong></p></th><td><p><strong>AI homework assistant:</strong> he or his parent uploads the homework (a photo or a file). Rafeeq reads the assignment and <strong>splits it into small steps</strong> (find the notebook, read page 15, short break, continue), then guides him through them one at a time.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>Homework checking</strong></p></th><td><p><strong>Homework photo checker:</strong> he takes a photo of his worksheet. Rafeeq reads his handwriting, checks each step of a math solution, and spots mistakes he keeps repeating (for example flipping a sign or forgetting to carry). Repeated mistakes are covered again in the next recap.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>Staying on task</strong></p></th><td><p><strong>Off-task detection during homework:</strong> with the opt-in home camera, gaze, head position and phone detection show when he drifts away from the task. The companion gives a gentle nudge. Video is processed on the device.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>Starting tasks</strong></p></th><td><p>Task initiation and task breakdown, shared capabilities reused across features (Section 10).</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>Chores</strong></p></th><td><p><strong>Gamified chores</strong>, for example a “Clean your room” challenge in small missions with points and badges. Some chores, such as cleaning the room or folding clothes, can be checked from a photo; other chores are games the parent confirms.</p></td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>UC3 · Child at school</h3><ul><li>A <strong>dedicated camera</strong> (provided with a school subscription, not the laptop camera) is mounted where the child is seen <strong>clearly and alone</strong>, without other students in view. A laptop camera can also work.</li><li>The camera detects <strong>fidgeting, leaving the seat, and whether the child is focused or has gone off focus</strong>.</li><li>Camera data is combined with the <strong>watch metrics</strong> (Section 4) to understand the child’s state.</li><li>Rafeeq gives the teacher a short message that explains <strong>what is happening, why, and how to respond</strong>. It is written for a <strong>class teacher</strong> or a <strong>shadow teacher</strong>.</li><li><strong>Overwhelm in class:</strong> the shared early overwhelm detection (UC1) also gives the teacher context when signs of overload rise in class.</li><li>Camera video is processed on the device. Only derived insights leave it. It needs consent from the parent and the school.</li><li><strong>Belongings reminders:</strong> calm reminders before leaving, so he does not forget his things at school.</li><li><strong>Missed content:</strong> moments when attention dropped are logged, so the content can be covered at home (UC2).</li></ul><h3>Example teacher guidance</h3><div class=\"proposal-table table-scroll\" role=\"region\" tabindex=\"0\" aria-label=\"Example teacher guidance — scrollable table\"><table><caption>Example teacher guidance</caption><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\"><p><strong>WHAT RAFEEQ SEES</strong></p></th><th scope=\"col\"><p><strong>WHAT IT MAY MEAN</strong></p></th><th scope=\"col\"><p><strong>WHAT THE TEACHER CAN DO</strong></p></th><th scope=\"col\"><p><strong>WHAT TO AVOID</strong></p></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>Fidgeting and heart rate rising</strong></p></th><td><p>The task is not stimulating enough, or the child is overloaded</p></td><td><p>Offer a movement job (hand out papers), a short break, or a change of activity</p></td><td><p>Telling him to sit still, or correcting him in front of the class</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>Gaze off the lesson for several minutes</strong></p></th><td><p>Attention has dropped; this is not defiance</p></td><td><p>Walk past quietly, use a private cue, restate one short step</p></td><td><p>Public reprimands, or repeating the same instruction louder</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>Left the seat repeatedly</strong></p></th><td><p>Restlessness he cannot easily control</p></td><td><p>Give a legitimate reason to move</p></td><td><p>Punishing movement</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>Stress signals high, then quiet and withdrawn</strong></p></th><td><p>Overwhelm</p></td><td><p>Offer a calm space or a choice of what to do next</p></td><td><p>Pressuring him to talk</p></td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>A shadow teacher gets one-to-one steps. A class teacher gets steps that work for the whole class. The tone is understanding and interactive, not a list of commands.</p><h3>UC4 · Parent</h3><ul><li><strong>Builds the routine:</strong> the parent enters the child’s normal daily chores, activities and sports. Rafeeq turns them into a clear, friendly schedule.</li><li><strong>Knows the day:</strong> the parent receives the complete daily schedule and the planning notifications for everything the child has today. The child only gets the essential reminder for what is happening now or next (UC1).</li><li><strong>Medication:</strong> the medication reminder goes to the <strong>parent</strong>, not the child. The parent confirms whether it was taken, and this goes into the psychiatrist’s report.</li><li><strong>Weekly feedback:</strong> some symptoms cannot be measured by the app. The parent answers a short weekly check-in so the symptom report is complete.</li><li><strong>Early-signs questionnaire</strong> for worried parents with no diagnosis yet.</li></ul><h3>UC5 · Psychiatrist</h3><ul><li>Receives a <strong>weekly report</strong> covering <strong>medication adherence and timing</strong> and the <strong>18 ADHD symptoms</strong> across the week (Section 8).</li><li>Sees how things <strong>changed over time</strong> instead of a single score, so they understand what happened between appointments.</li><li>Rafeeq does not diagnose. Assessment (for example DIVA) stays with the clinician.</li></ul></section><section class=\"panel proposal-section\" id=\"proposal-4\" aria-labelledby=\"heading-4\" hidden><h2 id=\"heading-4\">Assisting Tools metrics</h2><h3>Camera (opt-in, at school and during homework at home)</h3><div class=\"proposal-table table-scroll\" role=\"region\" tabindex=\"0\" aria-label=\"Camera (opt-in, at school and during homework at home) — scrollable table\"><table><caption>Camera (opt-in, at school and during homework at home)</caption><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\"><p><strong>SIGNAL</strong></p></th><th scope=\"col\"><p><strong>WHAT IT TELLS US</strong></p></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>Fidgeting</strong></p></th><td><p>Hand and foot movement, squirming</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>Movement and leaving the seat</strong></p></th><td><p>Restlessness, cannot stay seated</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>Focus and </strong><strong>off-focus</strong></p></th><td><p>Eye gaze and head position show whether he is on the task, in class or during homework</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>Phone or distraction in view</strong></p></th><td><p>Drifting away from homework (home)</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>Homework photo (taken by the child)</strong></p></th><td><p>Handwriting, math steps and repeated mistake patterns</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>Posture, yawning</strong></p></th><td><p>Tiredness or low engagement</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>Facial emotion (opt-in)</strong></p></th><td><p>Frustration, boredom, anxiety</p></td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Microphone (opt-in)</h3><div class=\"proposal-table table-scroll\" role=\"region\" tabindex=\"0\" aria-label=\"Microphone (opt-in) — scrollable table\"><table><caption>Microphone (opt-in)</caption><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\"><p><strong>SIGNAL</strong></p></th><th scope=\"col\"><p><strong>WHAT IT TELLS US</strong></p></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>Spoken answers</strong></p></th><td><p>His answer to a quiz or test question, graded by meaning</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>Reading aloud (Arabic and English)</strong></p></th><td><p>Reading accuracy (skipped or misread words) and reading pace</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>Voice tone</strong></p></th><td><p>Emotion, such as frustration or excitement</p></td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Smartwatch metrics</h3><p>Most health watches (Apple Watch, Galaxy Watch, Fitbit, Garmin and similar) report the metrics below. Some are not on every model, so we will confirm the chosen watch.</p><div class=\"proposal-table table-scroll\" role=\"region\" tabindex=\"0\" aria-label=\"Smartwatch metrics — scrollable table\"><table><caption>Smartwatch metrics</caption><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\"><p><strong>METRIC</strong></p></th><th scope=\"col\"><p><strong>WHAT IT TELLS US</strong></p></th><th scope=\"col\"><p><strong>USE IN RAFEEQ</strong></p></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>Heart rate and resting heart rate</strong></p></th><td><p>Arousal, stress, effort</p></td><td><p>Early overwhelm detection, teacher guidance</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>Heart rate variability (HRV)</strong></p></th><td><p>Stress and recovery</p></td><td><p>Stress and calm trends</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>Stress score</strong></p></th><td><p>Combined estimate of stress</p></td><td><p>Overload alerts</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>Movement (accelerometer) and steps</strong></p></th><td><p>Restlessness, activity</p></td><td><p>Hyperactivity symptoms, sports in the routine</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>Sleep duration and stages</strong></p></th><td><p>Sleep quality and consistency</p></td><td><p>Focus and sleep prediction, bedtime routine</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>Skin temperature (trend)</strong></p></th><td><p>Change from his own baseline</p></td><td><p>Context for sleep and illness</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>Blood oxygen (SpO₂) and breathing rate</strong></p></th><td><p>General state</p></td><td><p>Supporting context</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>Electrodermal activity (EDA, only some models)</strong></p></th><td><p>Emotional arousal</p></td><td><p>Stress and overwhelm detection</p></td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Watch data are trend indicators, not medical measurements.</p></section><section class=\"panel proposal-section\" id=\"proposal-5\" aria-labelledby=\"heading-5\" hidden><h2 id=\"heading-5\">Project summary</h2><ul><li><strong>What it is.</strong> An AI ecosystem for children with ADHD, shaped around each child, and organised into five use cases: the child throughout the day, at home and at school, the parent, and the psychiatrist.</li><li><strong>What the child gets.</strong> One agentic companion that remembers and adapts to him throughout the day, a smartwatch, an opt-in camera at school and during homework, lesson explanation and recap with multisensory tests (including reading aloud in Arabic and English), homework photo checking, homework and chore support, Focus Mode, calm-down support, personalised rewards and gentle reminders.</li><li><strong>Who sees what.</strong> Four roles (child, parent, teacher, doctor) each have their own access and see different information.</li><li><strong>How it works.</strong> Signals (app interactions, watch data, camera and voice with consent, homework photos, parent input) flow into one AI. It builds long-term memory and a prediction model, then sends each person only what they need, in plain language, with the reason behind it.</li><li><strong>Ground rules.</strong> Sensing is opt-in. Camera data is processed on the device. Language is always positive. Parents and the school consent to any classroom connection. Only a doctor can assess a child.</li><li><strong>What the project explores.</strong> Whether one AI system can fit itself to each child instead of treating every child with ADHD the same.</li></ul></section><section class=\"panel proposal-section\" id=\"proposal-6\" aria-labelledby=\"heading-6\" hidden><h2 id=\"heading-6\">Our solution</h2><p>Rafeeq connects three layers: what it senses, the AI that learns from it, and the people who see the result.</p><h3>Who uses it and what they get</h3><div class=\"proposal-table table-scroll\" role=\"region\" tabindex=\"0\" aria-label=\"Who uses it and what they get — scrollable table\"><table><caption>Who uses it and what they get</caption><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\"><p><strong>USER</strong></p></th><th scope=\"col\"><p><strong>WHAT RAFEEQ GIVES THEM</strong></p></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>Children with ADHD</strong></p></th><td><p>A companion through the day at home and at school: encouragement, focus help, lesson explanation and recap with tests he can answer his own way, homework checking, homework and chore support, and help when emotions run high.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>Parents and caregivers</strong></p></th><td><p>Routine builder, the complete day plan, medication confirmation, a weekly summary in plain language, and the early-signs questionnaire.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>Teachers</strong></p></th><td><p>Live guidance on how to understand and respond to the child, for class or shadow teachers.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>Doctors and clinicians</strong></p></th><td><p>A weekly report with medication and the 18 ADHD symptoms, as decision support for their own assessment.</p></td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>AI techniques used</h3><div class=\"proposal-table table-scroll\" role=\"region\" tabindex=\"0\" aria-label=\"AI techniques used — scrollable table\"><table><caption>AI techniques used</caption><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\"><p><strong>TECHNIQUE</strong></p></th><th scope=\"col\"><p><strong>WHAT IT DOES IN RAFEEQ</strong></p></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>Large language model</strong></p></th><td><p>Dialogue, coaching, storytelling, homework breakdown, lesson explanations and recaps</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>LLM agent with tool use and long-term memory</strong></p></th><td><p>The companion acts for the child (starts timers, adds tasks, opens a lesson, starts a quiz) and remembers his interests, struggles and what helped</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>Speech recognition (Arabic and English)</strong></p></th><td><p>Spoken answers in tests, and reading-aloud checks (skipped or misread words, reading pace)</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>Automatic answer grading</strong></p></th><td><p>Marks spoken, typed and handwritten answers by meaning, not exact wording</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>Handwriting recognition (OCR)</strong></p></th><td><p>Reads homework photos and handwritten answers in Arabic and English</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>Math solution checking</strong></p></th><td><p>Finds the step where a mistake happens and spots repeated mistake patterns</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>Speech emotion recognition</strong></p></th><td><p>Emotion from voice tone</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>Facial emotion recognition</strong></p></th><td><p>Expressions via opt-in camera</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>Eye-gaze estimation</strong></p></th><td><p>Visual attention, in class and during homework</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>Object detection</strong></p></th><td><p>A phone or other distraction in view during homework</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>Human pose estimation</strong></p></th><td><p>Restlessness and fidgeting</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>Reinforcement learning</strong></p></th><td><p>Best timing and type of nudge</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>Time-series forecasting</strong></p></th><td><p>Focus, sleep and stress patterns through the day, for focus and sleep prediction and early overwhelm detection</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>Recommendation system</strong></p></th><td><p>Activities, content, rewards</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>Knowledge graph</strong></p></th><td><p>Interests, routines, relationships</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>Explainable AI</strong></p></th><td><p>Why a recommendation was made</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>Content scoring model</strong></p></th><td><p>Overstimulation and educational value of videos and content</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>Video generation</strong></p></th><td><p>Recap videos of missed lessons and homework in the child’s own character</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>Personalisation</strong></p></th><td><p>Tone, animations and voice shaped to each child: look, pace and tone chosen from the child’s diagnosis and sensory profile</p></td></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"panel proposal-section\" id=\"proposal-7\" aria-labelledby=\"heading-7\" hidden><h2 id=\"heading-7\">What Rafeeq gives</h2><p>Each difficulty below is matched to the feature that answers it and to a way we could measure the change.</p><ul><li><strong>Inconsistent attention.</strong> Rafeeq learns the child’s best study times and steps in before a predicted dip. <em>Measured </em><em>by:</em> average focus duration over weeks.</li><li><strong>Emotional overload and meltdowns.</strong> Watch and voice signals catch rising overwhelm early. The Calm Down Menu guides regulation, and parents get context afterwards. <em>Measured </em><em>by:</em> number and length of escalations.</li><li><strong>Refusing to start tasks.</strong> The shared task initiation and task breakdown capabilities (Section 10) give one small first step at a time, and homework is split into small steps. <em>Measured </em><em>by:</em> time to start and completion rate.</li><li><strong>Drifting off during homework.</strong> Off-task detection notices when he drifts away and the companion nudges him gently. <em>Measured </em><em>by:</em> time on task and time to return after a nudge.</li><li><strong>Homework mistakes nobody catches.</strong> The homework photo checker finds the step where it went wrong and spots mistakes he keeps repeating. <em>Measured </em><em>by:</em> how often the same mistake types repeat over weeks.</li><li><strong>Tests that only suit some children.</strong> He answers by tapping, speaking, typing, writing or reading aloud, and his reading in Arabic and English is checked along the way. <em>Measured </em><em>by:</em> test completion rate, scores per answer mode, and reading accuracy and pace over weeks.</li><li><strong>Forgotten items and routines.</strong> Routine scheduling, personalised reminders and gamified chores. <em>Measured </em><em>by:</em> reminder response and missed routines.</li><li><strong>Everyone seeing a different picture.</strong> One data source feeds role-based views for child, parent, teacher and doctor. <em>Measured </em><em>by:</em> caregiver-reported usefulness.</li><li><strong>Missing part of a lesson.</strong> When attention drifts in class, the lesson is explained or recapped at home and then tested. <em>Measured </em><em>by:</em> homework completion and help needed on missed topics.</li><li><strong>Teachers noticing too late.</strong> Live guidance tells the teacher what the child needs and how to respond. <em>Measured </em><em>by:</em> teacher-reported usefulness and time from alert to break.</li><li><strong>Not knowing if things are getting better.</strong> A weekly report shows changes over time instead of a single score. <em>Measured by:</em> trends reviewed at doctor visits.</li><li><strong>Spotting signs late.</strong> A short questionnaire shows worried parents which symptoms to look for. It is a guide, not a diagnosis. <em>Measured </em><em>by:</em> how many families go on to book an assessment.</li><li><strong>A character that is too much, or too little.</strong> Tone, animations and level of stimulation are adapted to the child. <em>Measured </em><em>by:</em> engagement and overstimulation events.</li></ul><p>These outcomes are design goals. Any claim that Rafeeq improves attention or well-being has to be checked with user testing first.</p></section><section class=\"panel proposal-section\" id=\"proposal-8\" aria-labelledby=\"heading-8\" hidden><h2 id=\"heading-8\">The 18 ADHD symptoms: what we can and cannot measure</h2><p>The clinician report follows the DSM-5 symptom list. For each symptom, the table shows what Rafeeq can observe, what it cannot reliably measure on its own, and what needs input from the parent or teacher.</p><p>These measurements do not diagnose ADHD. Rafeeq shows how behaviour changes over the week. Assessment and diagnosis stay with the clinician.</p><p><strong>Summary:</strong> 8 symptoms Rafeeq can measure on its own, 7 it can measure only in part (parent or teacher input completes them), and 3 it cannot measure (mostly parent or teacher input).</p><div class=\"proposal-table table-scroll\" role=\"region\" tabindex=\"0\" aria-label=\"The 18 ADHD symptoms: what we can and cannot measure — scrollable table\"><table><caption>The 18 ADHD symptoms: what we can and cannot measure</caption><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\"><p><strong>#</strong></p></th><th scope=\"col\"><p><strong>SYMPTOM</strong></p></th><th scope=\"col\"><p><strong>LEVEL</strong></p></th><th scope=\"col\"><p><strong>RAFEEQ CAN OBSERVE</strong></p></th><th scope=\"col\"><p><strong>CANNOT RELIABLY MEASURE ALONE</strong></p></th><th scope=\"col\"><p><strong>NEEDS PARENT / TEACHER INPUT</strong></p></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope=\"row\" colspan=\"6\"><p><strong>INATTENTIVE</strong></p></th></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>1</strong></p></th><td><p>Poor listening</p></td><td><p>Partly</p></td><td><p>Whether he acts on spoken or on-screen instructions in the app, and how long it takes.</p></td><td><p>Whether he heard or understood, and listening in conversation.</p></td><td><p>Parent (home), teacher (class).</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>2</strong></p></th><td><p>Loses or misplaces items</p></td><td><p>Partly</p></td><td><p>Belongings checklist: reminders acknowledged or missed.</p></td><td><p>Whether an item was actually lost.</p></td><td><p>Parent, teacher.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>3</strong></p></th><td><p>Sidetracked by unimportant stimuli</p></td><td><p>Yes</p></td><td><p>Camera: gaze or head turning away, in class and during homework at home. Phone in view during homework. Focus Mode interruptions.</p></td><td><p>What distracted him, and why.</p></td><td><p>Teacher (optional context).</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>4</strong></p></th><td><p>Forgets daily activities</p></td><td><p>Yes</p></td><td><p>Routine items missed, or done only after a reminder.</p></td><td><p>Whether he forgot or chose not to.</p></td><td><p>Parent confirms.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>5</strong></p></th><td><p>Diminished attention span</p></td><td><p>Yes</p></td><td><p>Time on task from the app, camera (class and homework) and watch movement.</p></td><td><p>Attention off-screen, and hyperfocus on things he enjoys.</p></td><td><p>Parent, teacher (context).</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>6</strong></p></th><td><p>Does not finish schoolwork or follow instructions</p></td><td><p>Yes</p></td><td><p>Task, chunk and homework completion. Grades from a school platform once that integration is added (Section 14).</p></td><td><p>Work done off-app, and whether the cause is attention or difficulty.</p></td><td><p>Teacher confirms school work.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>7</strong></p></th><td><p>Avoids starting homework</p></td><td><p>Yes</p></td><td><p>Time from reminder to start. Skipped starts.</p></td><td><p>Why he avoids it (boredom, worry, difficulty).</p></td><td><p>Parent.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>8</strong></p></th><td><p>Careless mistakes, misses details</p></td><td><p>Partly</p></td><td><p>Quiz and test errors, grade patterns, and repeated mistake types from the homework photo checker.</p></td><td><p>Whether mistakes are careless or from lack of knowledge.</p></td><td><p>Teacher, parent.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>9</strong></p></th><td><p>Difficulty organising tasks</p></td><td><p>Partly</p></td><td><p>How he handles planned routines and chunked homework (order, skips).</p></td><td><p>Organisation of belongings, space and time outside the app.</p></td><td><p>Parent, teacher.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\" colspan=\"6\"><p><strong>HYPERACTIVE</strong></p></th></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>10</strong></p></th><td><p>Fidgets or squirms</p></td><td><p>Yes</p></td><td><p>Camera: hand and foot movement. Watch accelerometer.</p></td><td><p>Whether it is a symptom or normal movement.</p></td><td><p>Teacher (optional).</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>11</strong></p></th><td><p>Restlessness that is hard to control</p></td><td><p>Yes</p></td><td><p>Watch movement and heart rate. Camera motion.</p></td><td><p>Whether he can control it.</p></td><td><p>Parent.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>12</strong></p></th><td><p>Always “on the go”</p></td><td><p>Partly</p></td><td><p>Watch activity and steps through the day.</p></td><td><p>Whether he seems “driven”, and sport versus symptom.</p></td><td><p>Parent.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>13</strong></p></th><td><p>Cannot play quietly</p></td><td><p>Mostly input</p></td><td><p>Watch activity in leisure time (weak signal).</p></td><td><p>How he plays, and how loud.</p></td><td><p>Parent.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>14</strong></p></th><td><p>Cannot stay seated in class</p></td><td><p>Yes</p></td><td><p>School camera: leaving the seat, time seated.</p></td><td><p>Anything outside the camera view (home, other rooms).</p></td><td><p>Teacher confirms. Parent for home.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>15</strong></p></th><td><p>Overly talkative</p></td><td><p>Partly</p></td><td><p>Opt-in voice cues: amount of speech during app sessions.</p></td><td><p>Talkativeness in class and at home in general.</p></td><td><p>Parent, teacher.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\" colspan=\"6\"><p><strong>IMPULSIVE</strong></p></th></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>16</strong></p></th><td><p>Difficulty waiting his turn</p></td><td><p>Mostly input</p></td><td><p>Little. In-app waits (for example a countdown) as a weak sign.</p></td><td><p>Real-life turn-taking.</p></td><td><p>Parent, teacher.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>17</strong></p></th><td><p>Interrupts or intrudes on others</p></td><td><p>Mostly input</p></td><td><p>Voice cues in app sessions only.</p></td><td><p>Offline conversations and play.</p></td><td><p>Parent, teacher.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>18</strong></p></th><td><p>Blurts out answers before the question ends</p></td><td><p>Partly</p></td><td><p>Answering before a question ends in app quizzes, including spoken answers.</p></td><td><p>Blurting in class.</p></td><td><p>Teacher, parent.</p></td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Level: Yes = Rafeeq can measure it on its own. Partly = Rafeeq sees part of it and the parent or teacher completes it. Mostly input = the report relies on the parent or teacher.</p><ul><li>The uploaded DSM-5 table lists 17 symptoms. The full DSM-5 list has 18, and the missing one is “difficulty organising tasks and activities” (#9).</li><li>DSM-5 asks for symptoms in two or more settings. The school camera alone is not enough, so the weekly parent check-in covers home, together with the opt-in homework camera. Teacher input is an optional short form, if the school agrees.</li><li>Symptoms are shown as <strong>change over the week</strong>, not a single score.</li></ul></section><section class=\"panel proposal-section\" id=\"proposal-9\" aria-labelledby=\"heading-9\" hidden><h2 id=\"heading-9\">Features</h2><div id=\"discover-features\"></div><details class=\"proposal-specifications\"><summary>Full proposal feature specifications</summary><p>Features are grouped by use case. Shared functional capabilities are in Section 10, extra features are in Section 11, and the rules that apply to every feature are in Section 12. The requirements are in Section 13, and ideas for later are in Section 14.</p><h3>Key features</h3><div class=\"proposal-table table-scroll\" role=\"region\" tabindex=\"0\" aria-label=\"Key features — scrollable table\"><table><caption>Key features</caption><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\"><p><strong>FEATURE</strong></p></th><th scope=\"col\"><p><strong>WHAT IT DOES</strong></p></th><th scope=\"col\"><p><strong>WHY IT STANDS OUT</strong></p></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope=\"row\" colspan=\"3\"><p><strong>UC1 · CHILD THROUGHOUT THE DAY</strong></p></th></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>Personalized agentic companion</strong></p></th><td><p>Rafeeq features one consistent 3D animated character for all children, with a personalized interaction experience for each child. The character adapts its voice, tone, response speed, and communication style to the child’s preferences. A short onboarding game helps identify the child’s preferred sound, light, and texture experiences. The companion responds to the child’s mood, celebrates achievements, and encourages them through friendly invitations rather than commands. Children can unlock clothes, hats, accessories, and pets as rewards, allowing them to customize their character’s appearance while keeping the main character the same for everyone.</p><p><strong>Agent with long-term memory.</strong> The companion is an AI agent. It remembers the child’s interests, what he found hard and what helped him before, and it can act for him: start a timer, add a task, open a lesson or start a quiz, always within the parent’s settings. Its replies pass through safety filters suitable for children. It goes with the child throughout the day and connects the other Rafeeq features, such as lessons, homework, Focus Mode and the Calm Down Menu.</p></td><td><p>Most companion apps give every child the same character and tone, and only chat. Rafeeq’s companion remembers him and can act.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>Early overwhelm detection</strong></p></th><td><p>Heart rate, movement, sleep and inactivity from the watch, with opt-in voice and camera cues. When signs rise, the companion steps in, offers the Calm Down Menu, and the teacher or parent gets context. It works across settings (home, school and in between), depending on which devices are available and on consent.</p></td><td><p>Links body signals to a response. Others only cue the child to refocus.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>Personalized rewards and badges</strong></p></th><td><p>Digital rewards and achievement badges for personal progress, such as a 7-day routine streak, with progress tracking and positive feedback. One reward system is reused across homework, chores, routines, lessons and other activities, and rewards include customisation items for the companion (clothes, hats, accessories, pets). It is separate from the companion itself.</p></td><td><p>Motivates without physical rewards or competition.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>Personalized focus and sleep prediction</strong></p></th><td><p>Sleep metrics from the watch, together with his sleep, schedule and interaction history, show his <strong>best focus time</strong> and a good bedtime, and reveal patterns such as focus dropping at 5 PM on Mondays. Study time is recommended around these windows. A light simulation of his habits (a behavioural digital twin) tests which interventions will probably work, and the model keeps adapting as his longitudinal history grows.</p></td><td><p>Study and routine follow his own rhythm, and focus loss is prevented instead of reacted to.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>Calm Down Menu</strong></p></th><td><p>When overwhelmed, the child opens a menu to name the feeling, pick a calming activity, take a short break, or follow a step-by-step exercise. It includes a calm mini-space (breathing animation, soft sounds, fidget-style interaction). He can open it himself at any time, and the companion offers it when early overwhelm detection sees signs rising. Activities are personalised to what helps him.</p></td><td><p>Teaches regulation, not just “calm down”.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>Focus Mode</strong></p></th><td><p>A study mode that silences distracting notifications and keeps the child in the current task, with focus sessions and a visual timer. He can turn it on whenever he needs it, at home, at school or anywhere else. It combines with task initiation and task breakdown (Section 10) and, during homework, with off-task detection.</p></td><td><p>Helps him concentrate wherever he is, not only at a desk at home.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>Personalized notifications and reminders</strong></p></th><td><p>The child gets only the essential reminders: what he is doing <strong>right now</strong>, what is next, and gentle transition reminders before an activity changes. Timing and presentation (sound, animation, wording, how early) are personalised to him, and notifications are kept minimal so they never overwhelm. The complete daily schedule goes to the parent (UC4).</p></td><td><p>Keeps the child calm and on track without flooding him with alerts.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\" colspan=\"3\"><p><strong>UC2 · CHILD AT HOME</strong></p></th></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>Lesson explanation</strong></p></th><td><p>When he missed content or a lesson is new, the companion explains it step by step in different forms (text, speech, video, visuals). Small quizzes come during the explanation, and a <strong>comprehension test</strong> after it. Every quiz and test is <strong>multisensory</strong>: he can choose an answer (MCQ), say it aloud, type it, write it by hand, or drag and build. Some questions ask him to <strong>read aloud</strong>, so his reading in Arabic and English is tested too.</p></td><td><p>Missed content is explained by his own companion, at home, when he is ready, and tested in the way he answers best.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>Lesson recap and revision</strong></p></th><td><p>Revises a lesson with a <strong>summary of the main points</strong>, then tests him on it with the same multisensory answer options, including reading aloud in Arabic and English. Mistakes he keeps making (from tests and homework photos) come back in the recap.</p></td><td><p>A quick refresher, separate from learning something new.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>AI homework assistant</strong></p></th><td><p>The child or parent uploads the homework (a photo or a file). Rafeeq reads the assignment, <strong>divides it into small, manageable steps</strong> ordered around his best focus times, and guides him through them one at a time, using the shared task initiation and task breakdown capabilities (Section 10). Connecting to school platforms such as Google Classroom is a possible future integration (Section 14), not part of the first build.</p></td><td><p>Turns a whole assignment into one clear next step.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>Homework photo checker</strong></p></th><td><p>He takes a photo of his worksheet. Rafeeq reads his handwriting (Arabic and English), checks each step of a math solution, and spots mistakes he keeps repeating, such as flipping a sign or forgetting to carry. Feedback is gentle and points to the step to look at again, rather than giving the answer.</p></td><td><p>Catches the mistake he keeps making, not just the wrong answer.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>Off-task detection during homework</strong></p></th><td><p>With the opt-in home camera, gaze, head position and phone detection show when he drifts away from homework. The companion gives a gentle nudge, or suggests a break if he has been working for a long time. Video is processed on the device and never leaves it.</p></td><td><p>Notices drifting early and nudges gently, without a parent watching over him.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>Gamified chores</strong></p></th><td><p>A chore becomes a small challenge in missions, with progress, feedback, points and badges. Certain chores, <strong>such as cleaning the room or folding clothes</strong>, can be supported by camera-based verification from a photo the child takes. These are examples, not a complete list:</p><p><strong>Clean your room:</strong> make the bed, pick up clothes from the floor, put toys away, organise the desk, then a final check of the whole room (a “before” and an “after” photo).</p><p><strong>Fold clothes:</strong> the photo shows whether the clothes are folded.</p><p>Other chores, such as clearing the dishes or taking out the trash, are <strong>games</strong> the child marks done and the parent confirms. More chores and verification methods may be explored through further research and added as future enhancements (Section 14). The parent can always override the AI’s result.</p></td><td><p>Makes everyday responsibilities engaging, and checks the result instead of only trusting a tap.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\" colspan=\"3\"><p><strong>UC3 · CHILD AT SCHOOL</strong></p></th></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>Classroom attention and engagement guidance</strong></p></th><td><p>A dedicated single-student camera (fidgeting, movement, focus) combined with watch metrics. Rafeeq tells the teacher <strong>what is happening, why, how to respond and what to avoid</strong>, for a class or shadow teacher. An in-app nudge to the child is tried first. Moments when attention dropped are logged, so the missed lesson content can be covered at home (UC2). In class, it also uses the shared early overwhelm detection (UC1).</p></td><td><p>Tells the teacher what to do in the moment, with understanding, not just commands.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>Belongings reminders</strong></p></th><td><p>Calm reminders to pack his things before leaving school.</p></td><td><p>Reduces lost items.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\" colspan=\"3\"><p><strong>UC4 · PARENT</strong></p></th></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>Routine and chores builder</strong></p></th><td><p>The parent enters normal daily chores, sports and activities. Rafeeq formats them into a clear schedule.</p></td><td><p>Parents get a schedule without building it by hand.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>Day plan and planning notifications</strong></p></th><td><p>The parent receives the complete daily schedule and all planning and management notifications, so the parent always knows what is planned for the day without nagging the child. The child does not get every notification: he only gets the essential reminders for what is happening now or next (UC1, personalized notifications and reminders), so he is not overwhelmed.</p></td><td><p>Keeps the child calm and the parent informed.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>Medication check</strong></p></th><td><p>The medication reminder goes to the parent, who confirms whether it was taken and when. This feeds the clinician report.</p></td><td><p>Keeps medication data accurate without pressure on the child.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>Weekly parent check-in</strong></p></th><td><p>A short weekly questionnaire for symptoms the app cannot measure (Section 8). It also produces a conversation guide for the family.</p></td><td><p>Completes the symptom picture for the doctor.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>Insight summaries and trends</strong></p></th><td><p>Best focus windows, meltdown triggers and which reminder styles work, delivered daily and weekly as insights, not logs. Includes reading progress and repeated homework mistakes.</p></td><td><p>Parents get what to do next, not a spreadsheet.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>Early-signs questionnaire</strong></p></th><td><p>A short questionnaire explains symptoms in plain language and suggests whether to talk to a doctor.</p></td><td><p>A first step before a clinic visit. It is a guide, not a diagnosis.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\" colspan=\"3\"><p><strong>UC5 · PSYCHIATRIST</strong></p></th></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>Weekly clinical report</strong></p></th><td><p>Medication adherence and timing, the 18 ADHD symptoms through the week (Section 8), trends over weeks and months (better, same or worse), and patterns between appointments. The report can be <strong>exported</strong> (PDF or FHIR-style, to fit the clinic’s records) and viewed <strong>weekly, biweekly or monthly</strong>.</p></td><td><p>Shows change over time, not one score or a pile of raw data.</p></td></tr></tbody></table></div></details></section><section class=\"panel proposal-section\" id=\"proposal-10\" aria-labelledby=\"heading-10\" hidden><h2 id=\"heading-10\">Shared functional capabilities</h2><p>These are not separate features. They are building blocks that several features reuse, such as the AI homework assistant, gamified chores, Focus Mode, lesson activities and daily routines. Each is described once here and referenced from the features that use it.</p><div class=\"proposal-table table-scroll\" role=\"region\" tabindex=\"0\" aria-label=\"Shared functional capabilities — scrollable table\"><table><caption>Shared functional capabilities</caption><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\"><p><strong>CAPABILITY</strong></p></th><th scope=\"col\"><p><strong>WHAT IT DOES AND WHERE IT IS REUSED</strong></p></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>Task initiation</strong></p></th><td><p>Helps the child begin an activity by offering a manageable first step, encouragement and a choice of where to begin. A short countdown starts the task, and the companion starts alongside him, celebrates starting, adapts the task if he struggles, and tracks time to start to monitor progress. Starting is one of the hardest parts of ADHD, and this will be tested with children and parents. <strong>Used </strong><strong>in:</strong> the AI homework assistant, gamified chores, Focus Mode, lesson activities and daily routines.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>Task breakdown</strong></p></th><td><p>Divides a complex activity into smaller, manageable steps (for example: find the notebook, read page 15, short break, continue) and presents one instruction at a time, so the child never faces the whole task at once. <strong>Used </strong><strong>in:</strong> the AI homework assistant, gamified chores (missions), Focus Mode, lesson activities and daily routines.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>Multisensory tests and reading-aloud checks</strong></p></th><td><p>Every quiz and test, in the explanation and the recap, lets him answer by choosing (MCQ), saying it aloud, typing, writing by hand, or dragging and building. Spoken and written answers are graded by meaning, not exact wording. Some questions ask him to <strong>read aloud</strong> in Arabic or English: Rafeeq catches skipped and misread words, measures reading pace and tracks reading progress over the weeks.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>Visual timers and gradual transitions</strong></p></th><td><p>A visual timer for each task shows time passed and time left. Warnings come in stages (10 minutes, 5, 2) before the next activity, so transitions are gradual instead of “stop now”. This supports time blindness and hard transitions. <strong>Used in:</strong> Focus Mode, daily routines, the AI homework assistant, gamified chores, lesson activities, and the child’s transition reminders.</p></td></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"panel proposal-section\" id=\"proposal-11\" aria-labelledby=\"heading-11\" hidden><h2 id=\"heading-11\">Additional features</h2><p>These support the key features but are not the core of the first build.</p><div class=\"proposal-table table-scroll\" role=\"region\" tabindex=\"0\" aria-label=\"Additional features — scrollable table\"><table><caption>Additional features</caption><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\"><p><strong>FEATURE</strong></p></th><th scope=\"col\"><p><strong>WHAT IT DOES AND WHY IT HELPS</strong></p></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>AI video and channel checker</strong></p></th><td><p>The parent pastes a YouTube link or a channel. Rafeeq analyses pacing, scene changes, loudness, flashing lights and language, then returns an Overstimulation Score, an Educational Score and a verdict (watch now, watch with a break, or not today). It uses the child’s state, sensory profile and bedtime, and always shows the reason. Parents set the thresholds and have the final say.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>Context-aware screen time</strong></p></th><td><p>Limits depend on homework status, total exposure, current overstimulation and bedtime, not a fixed timer. Content is grouped into a daily Balance Score. When overload is detected, the device lowers brightness, mutes notifications or switches to grayscale. The app also supports healthy transitions away from the screen.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>Personalised</strong><strong> stories and focus games</strong></p></th><td><p>Stories and games built around his interests, so attention practice feels like play.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>ADHD resources for parents and teachers</strong></p></th><td><p>Short guides on what ADHD is and how to interact with the child, to ease guilt and confusion.</p></td></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"panel proposal-section\" id=\"proposal-12\" aria-labelledby=\"heading-12\" hidden><h2 id=\"heading-12\">General guidelines</h2><p>These rules apply to every feature and every use case.</p><h3>Tone and communication</h3><ul><li><strong>Positive-only language.</strong> No shaming, and no comparison with classmates or other children.</li><li><strong>Choices, not orders.</strong> For example, “Do you want to start with your books or your clothes?”</li><li><strong>Short, calm, clear instructions.</strong> One instruction at a time. Reminders are gentle and never stressful.</li><li><strong>No aggressive reminders</strong>, pressure or stressful notifications. Off-task nudges are gentle invitations, never warnings.</li></ul><h3>Support, not control</h3><ul><li><strong>Support the child, do not control them.</strong> Rafeeq helps the child regulate instead of constantly telling them what to do.</li><li><strong>Teach independence.</strong> Rafeeq teaches strategies and gradually reduces unnecessary help, so the child does not depend on the companion. The homework checker points to the step to look at again instead of giving the answer.</li><li><strong>Focus on regulation.</strong> The goal is not to remove ADHD behaviours but to help the child understand and manage them. Movement, talking or emotional intensity are signs of low stimulation or hard self-regulation, not misbehaviour.</li><li><strong>Hyperfocus detection.</strong> Rafeeq notices long stillness, ignored reminders or missed breaks, and suggests a stretch or a glass of water.</li></ul><h3>Progress and rewards</h3><ul><li><strong>No comparison.</strong> Progress is measured against the child’s own past performance. No rankings.</li><li><strong>Rewards for specific tasks</strong> completed through the day. They are mainly digital and experience-based (accessories, outfits, hats, pets, badges) and personal.</li><li><strong>Rewards must not take over.</strong> Rafeeq must not become a system that constantly demands or hands out rewards.</li><li><strong>Not every interaction is a score.</strong> Weekly summaries focus on improvements, difficulties, patterns, task completion, emotional regulation and routine consistency.</li></ul><h3>Engagement</h3><ul><li><strong>Keep tasks engaging</strong> with gamification, visual feedback, games and companion interaction.</li><li><strong>Break long activities</strong> into manageable parts.</li><li><strong>Present information in different forms</strong> (text, short summaries, visuals, interactive), then test understanding in different ways too (tap, speak, type, write by hand, read aloud).</li><li><strong>Multiple modes:</strong> text and speech modes, plus a voice-only mode for younger children and low-literacy users.</li></ul><h3>Companion and personalisation</h3><ul><li><strong>One consistent main character.</strong> Tone and reactions change with the child’s needs, but he never feels like a different system.</li><li><strong>Personalise</strong><strong>.</strong> The same strategy does not work for every child. Rafeeq keeps a long-term child profile (favourite animal, best friend, hard subjects, sensory sensitivities, attention span, reading level, mistakes he often makes), so every conversation feels personal.</li><li><strong>The agent acts within limits.</strong> The companion only uses the actions the parent allows, and never changes medication, settings or schedules on its own.</li></ul><h3>Routine, time and screens</h3><ul><li><strong>Structured, simple, short, predictable routines.</strong></li><li><strong>Gradual transitions.</strong> Warnings before ending an activity (10, 5, 2 minutes) instead of “stop now”.</li><li><strong>Allow for unexpected days.</strong> The plan can change when a day goes differently.</li><li><strong>Do not keep the child on the screen.</strong> Screen breaks are managed so they do not make it harder to return to a task.</li></ul><h3>Parents, teachers and clinicians</h3><ul><li><strong>Strong parent controls.</strong> Parents can adjust routines and decide how much help Rafeeq gives. The child must never feel constantly watched, controlled or evaluated.</li><li><strong>Support the whole ecosystem.</strong> Parents may feel guilty or unsure, and teachers may not know how to respond. Rafeeq offers practical guidance, not only generic ADHD information.</li><li><strong>Explain, don’t command.</strong> Teacher guidance covers why the child behaves this way, how to respond, what helps and what makes it worse.</li><li><strong>Concise reports.</strong> Show patterns and changes rather than raw data.</li></ul><h3>Scope and safety</h3><ul><li><strong>Target age</strong> is children up to about 12 years old. Older children’s needs and independence differ.</li><li><strong>No diagnosis.</strong> Rafeeq is a support tool. Assessment stays with the clinician.</li><li><strong>Consent and privacy.</strong> Sensing is opt-in. Camera video, at school and at home, is processed on the device. Voice recordings and homework photos are used only for the child’s own feedback. Parents and the school consent to any classroom connection.</li></ul></section><section class=\"panel proposal-section\" id=\"proposal-13\" aria-labelledby=\"heading-13\" hidden><h2 id=\"heading-13\">Functional and non-functional requirements</h2><p>The requirements below come from the features in Sections 9, 10 and 11 and the rules in Section 12. Priority shows what the first build needs: <strong>Must</strong> is required for the first build, <strong>Should</strong> is planned for it if time allows, and <strong>Could</strong> is optional.</p><h3>Functional requirements</h3><div class=\"proposal-table table-scroll\" role=\"region\" tabindex=\"0\" aria-label=\"Functional requirements — scrollable table\"><table><caption>Functional requirements</caption><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\"><p><strong>ID</strong></p></th><th scope=\"col\"><p><strong>REQUIREMENT: THE SYSTEM SHALL…</strong></p></th><th scope=\"col\"><p><strong>FEATURE</strong></p></th><th scope=\"col\"><p><strong>PRIORITY</strong></p></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope=\"row\" colspan=\"4\"><p><strong>GENERAL · ACCOUNTS AND ROLES</strong></p></th></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>FR-01</strong></p></th><td><p>Let a parent create an account, add a child profile and give consent for each sensor (camera, microphone, watch) separately.</p></td><td><p>Accounts and consent</p></td><td><p>Must</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>FR-02</strong></p></th><td><p>Provide four roles (child, parent, teacher, psychiatrist), each with its own view and access to different information.</p></td><td><p>Role-based views</p></td><td><p>Must</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>FR-03</strong></p></th><td><p>Connect to a supported smartwatch and receive heart rate, HRV, movement, steps and sleep data.</p></td><td><p>Watch metrics</p></td><td><p>Must</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>FR-04</strong></p></th><td><p>Keep one long-term child profile (interests, sensory profile, hard subjects, reading level, repeated mistakes) shared by every feature.</p></td><td><p>Shared memory</p></td><td><p>Must</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\" colspan=\"4\"><p><strong>UC1 · CHILD THROUGHOUT THE DAY</strong></p></th></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>FR-05</strong></p></th><td><p>Provide one 3D animated companion that adapts voice, tone, response speed and style to the child, set up through a short onboarding game.</p></td><td><p>Personalized agentic companion</p></td><td><p>Must</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>FR-06</strong></p></th><td><p>Let the companion act for the child (start a timer, add a task, open a lesson, start a quiz) only within the actions the parent allows.</p></td><td><p>Personalized agentic companion</p></td><td><p>Must</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>FR-07</strong></p></th><td><p>Filter every companion reply through child-safety checks.</p></td><td><p>Personalized agentic companion</p></td><td><p>Must</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>FR-08</strong></p></th><td><p>Detect rising overwhelm from watch signals (and voice and camera cues when enabled) in any setting where the devices are available and consent is given, and trigger the companion and a context message to the teacher or parent.</p></td><td><p>Early overwhelm detection</p></td><td><p>Must</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>FR-09</strong></p></th><td><p>Award personalised digital rewards and badges (accessories, outfits, pets, streaks) based on the child’s own progress, through one reward system shared by homework, chores, routines, lessons and other activities, and track his progress.</p></td><td><p>Personalized rewards and badges</p></td><td><p>Must</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>FR-10</strong></p></th><td><p>Estimate the child’s best focus time and a good bedtime from sleep data and his history, recommend study times around them, and predict focus drops from sleep, schedule and interaction history, testing likely interventions in a simulation of the child’s habits.</p></td><td><p>Personalized focus and sleep prediction</p></td><td><p>Should</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>FR-11</strong></p></th><td><p>Provide a Calm Down Menu the child can open at any time to name a feeling and choose a calming activity, break or guided exercise, and offer it when early overwhelm is detected.</p></td><td><p>Calm Down Menu</p></td><td><p>Must</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>FR-12</strong></p></th><td><p>Provide a Focus Mode, available whenever the child needs it, that silences distracting notifications, runs focus sessions with a visual timer, and works with task initiation, task breakdown and off-task detection.</p></td><td><p>Focus Mode</p></td><td><p>Should</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>FR-13</strong></p></th><td><p>Send the child only essential reminders for his current and next activity, with gentle transition reminders, using personalised timing and presentation and keeping notifications minimal.</p></td><td><p>Personalized notifications and reminders</p></td><td><p>Must</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\" colspan=\"4\"><p><strong>UC2 · CHILD AT HOME</strong></p></th></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>FR-14</strong></p></th><td><p>Explain a new or missed lesson step by step in the child’s preferred form (text, speech, video, visuals), with small quizzes during it and a comprehension test after it.</p></td><td><p>Lesson explanation</p></td><td><p>Must</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>FR-15</strong></p></th><td><p>Recap a lesson as a summary of the main points followed by a test, and bring back mistakes the child keeps making.</p></td><td><p>Lesson recap and revision</p></td><td><p>Must</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>FR-16</strong></p></th><td><p>Accept test answers by MCQ, speech, typing, handwriting, and drag and build, and grade spoken, typed and written answers by meaning.</p></td><td><p>Multisensory tests and reading-aloud checks</p></td><td><p>Must</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>FR-17</strong></p></th><td><p>Include read-aloud questions in Arabic and English, detect skipped and misread words, measure reading pace and track reading progress over weeks.</p></td><td><p>Multisensory tests and reading-aloud checks</p></td><td><p>Must</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>FR-18</strong></p></th><td><p>Let the child or parent upload homework manually (a photo or a file), read the assignment, divide it into small, manageable steps ordered around the child’s best focus times, and guide the child through them using the shared task initiation and task breakdown capabilities.</p></td><td><p>AI homework assistant</p></td><td><p>Must</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>FR-19</strong></p></th><td><p>Read a photo of a worksheet (Arabic and English handwriting), check each step of a math solution, point to the step to look at again without giving the answer, and record repeated mistake types.</p></td><td><p>Homework photo checker</p></td><td><p>Must</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>FR-20</strong></p></th><td><p>Detect off-task behaviour during homework from gaze, head position and a phone in view, then send a gentle nudge or suggest a break.</p></td><td><p>Off-task detection</p></td><td><p>Must</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>FR-21</strong></p></th><td><p>Turn chores into small missions with progress, feedback, points and badges. Chores without camera-based verification are games the child marks done and the parent confirms.</p></td><td><p>Gamified chores</p></td><td><p>Should</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>FR-22</strong></p></th><td><p>Support camera-based verification for certain chores from photos the child takes, such as <strong>clean your room</strong> (bed made, no clothes on the floor, toys put away, desk organised, and a before-and-after check of the whole room) and <strong>fold clothes</strong> (clothes folded). Award points only when the check passes, and let the parent override the result.</p></td><td><p>Gamified chores</p></td><td><p>Should</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\" colspan=\"4\"><p><strong>UC3 · CHILD AT SCHOOL</strong></p></th></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>FR-23</strong></p></th><td><p>Detect from the school camera whether the child is focused or off focus, fidgeting, or leaving his seat.</p></td><td><p>Classroom attention guidance</p></td><td><p>Must</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>FR-24</strong></p></th><td><p>Combine camera signals with watch metrics to estimate the child’s state.</p></td><td><p>Classroom attention guidance</p></td><td><p>Must</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>FR-25</strong></p></th><td><p>Send the teacher a short message explaining what is happening, why, how to respond and what to avoid, adapted to a class or shadow teacher.</p></td><td><p>Classroom attention guidance</p></td><td><p>Must</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>FR-26</strong></p></th><td><p>Try an in-app nudge to the child before alerting the teacher.</p></td><td><p>Classroom attention guidance</p></td><td><p>Should</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>FR-27</strong></p></th><td><p>Log moments when attention dropped in class so the missed content can be covered at home.</p></td><td><p>Missed content</p></td><td><p>Must</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>FR-28</strong></p></th><td><p>Send calm belongings reminders before the child leaves school.</p></td><td><p>Belongings reminders</p></td><td><p>Should</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\" colspan=\"4\"><p><strong>UC4 · PARENT</strong></p></th></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>FR-29</strong></p></th><td><p>Let the parent enter chores, activities and sports, and turn them into a clear daily schedule.</p></td><td><p>Routine and chores builder</p></td><td><p>Must</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>FR-30</strong></p></th><td><p>Send the parent the complete daily schedule and all planning notifications, while the child receives only the essential reminders in FR-13.</p></td><td><p>Day plan and planning notifications</p></td><td><p>Must</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>FR-31</strong></p></th><td><p>Send medication reminders to the parent and record whether and when the medication was taken.</p></td><td><p>Medication check</p></td><td><p>Must</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>FR-32</strong></p></th><td><p>Provide a short weekly check-in for symptoms the app cannot measure, and a conversation guide for the family.</p></td><td><p>Weekly parent check-in</p></td><td><p>Must</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>FR-33</strong></p></th><td><p>Deliver daily and weekly insights (focus windows, triggers, effective reminder styles, reading progress, repeated mistakes).</p></td><td><p>Insight summaries and trends</p></td><td><p>Should</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>FR-34</strong></p></th><td><p>Provide an early-signs questionnaire that explains symptoms in plain language and suggests whether to see a doctor, without giving a diagnosis.</p></td><td><p>Early-signs questionnaire</p></td><td><p>Should</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\" colspan=\"4\"><p><strong>UC5 · PSYCHIATRIST</strong></p></th></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>FR-35</strong></p></th><td><p>Generate a weekly clinical report with medication adherence and timing, the 18 DSM-5 symptoms over the week, and trends between appointments.</p></td><td><p>Weekly clinical report</p></td><td><p>Must</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>FR-36</strong></p></th><td><p>Let the clinician view the report weekly, biweekly or monthly and export it as PDF or in a FHIR-style format.</p></td><td><p>Weekly clinical report</p></td><td><p>Should</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\" colspan=\"4\"><p><strong>SHARED FUNCTIONAL CAPABILITIES</strong></p></th></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>FR-37</strong></p></th><td><p>Guide the child to start a task with a manageable first step, encouragement, a choice of where to begin and a short countdown, and record the time to start.</p></td><td><p>Task initiation</p></td><td><p>Must</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>FR-38</strong></p></th><td><p>Divide complex activities into smaller, manageable steps and present one instruction at a time.</p></td><td><p>Task breakdown</p></td><td><p>Must</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>FR-39</strong></p></th><td><p>Show a visual timer for each task, and give staged warnings (10, 5 and 2 minutes) and gradual transitions before the next activity.</p></td><td><p>Visual timers and gradual transitions</p></td><td><p>Must</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\" colspan=\"4\"><p><strong>ADDITIONAL FEATURES</strong></p></th></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>FR-40</strong></p></th><td><p>Analyse a YouTube video or channel and return an Overstimulation Score, an Educational Score, a verdict and the reason, using parent-set thresholds.</p></td><td><p>AI video and channel checker</p></td><td><p>Should</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>FR-41</strong></p></th><td><p>Set screen-time limits from homework status, exposure, overstimulation and bedtime, and calm the device (brightness, mute, grayscale) when overload is detected.</p></td><td><p>Context-aware screen time</p></td><td><p>Could</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>FR-42</strong></p></th><td><p>Generate stories and focus games around the child’s interests.</p></td><td><p>Personalised stories and games</p></td><td><p>Could</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>FR-43</strong></p></th><td><p>Provide short ADHD guides for parents and teachers.</p></td><td><p>ADHD resources</p></td><td><p>Could</p></td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Non-functional requirements</h3><div class=\"proposal-table table-scroll\" role=\"region\" tabindex=\"0\" aria-label=\"Non-functional requirements — scrollable table\"><table><caption>Non-functional requirements</caption><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\"><p><strong>ID</strong></p></th><th scope=\"col\"><p><strong>CATEGORY</strong></p></th><th scope=\"col\"><p><strong>REQUIREMENT</strong></p></th><th scope=\"col\"><p><strong>HOW WE CHECK IT</strong></p></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>NFR-01</strong></p></th><td><p>Privacy</p></td><td><p>Camera video, at school and at home, is processed on the device. Only derived insights leave it. Voice recordings, homework photos and chore photos are used only for the child’s own feedback.</p></td><td><p>Network inspection shows no raw video leaving the device.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>NFR-02</strong></p></th><td><p>Consent</p></td><td><p>Every sensor is off until the parent turns it on. Classroom use also needs the school’s consent. Consent can be withdrawn at any time.</p></td><td><p>Sensors stay inactive without consent in tests.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>NFR-03</strong></p></th><td><p>Security</p></td><td><p>Data is encrypted in transit and at rest, and each role sees only its own data.</p></td><td><p>TLS everywhere; role-access tests pass.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>NFR-04</strong></p></th><td><p>Performance</p></td><td><p>Off-task and classroom signals reach the companion or teacher quickly enough to act on.</p></td><td><p>Alert in under 5 seconds from detection.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>NFR-05</strong></p></th><td><p>Performance</p></td><td><p>The companion starts replying fast enough to keep a child engaged.</p></td><td><p>First response in under 3 seconds.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>NFR-06</strong></p></th><td><p>Accuracy</p></td><td><p>Detection and grading models meet agreed accuracy before release, and are reported per answer mode and per language.</p></td><td><p>Accuracy and F1 on held-out test sets.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>NFR-07</strong></p></th><td><p>Usability</p></td><td><p>Interfaces suit children up to about 12: one instruction at a time, large touch targets, and a voice-only mode for younger and low-literacy users.</p></td><td><p>Task success in usability tests with children.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>NFR-08</strong></p></th><td><p>Language</p></td><td><p>The app, speech recognition, handwriting reading and read-aloud checks work in Arabic and English, including right-to-left layout.</p></td><td><p>Both languages pass the same test set.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>NFR-09</strong></p></th><td><p>Tone and safety</p></td><td><p>All messages are positive, give choices instead of orders, and never compare the child with others. Companion replies pass child-safety filters.</p></td><td><p>Content review and filter test set.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>NFR-10</strong></p></th><td><p>Explainability</p></td><td><p>Every recommendation, alert, score and verdict shows the reason behind it.</p></td><td><p>Each output has a visible reason.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>NFR-11</strong></p></th><td><p>Clinical limits</p></td><td><p>The system never diagnoses or changes medication. The report is decision support for the clinician.</p></td><td><p>Review of all report and questionnaire wording.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>NFR-12</strong></p></th><td><p>Agent control</p></td><td><p>The agent can only use actions the parent allows, and never changes medication, settings or schedules on its own.</p></td><td><p>Blocked actions are refused in tests.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>NFR-13</strong></p></th><td><p>Reliability</p></td><td><p>Core child features (timers, routine, Calm Down Menu) work offline and sync later.</p></td><td><p>Offline test on the device.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>NFR-14</strong></p></th><td><p>Compatibility</p></td><td><p>The app runs on Android and iOS and supports the main smartwatch platforms.</p></td><td><p>Tested on at least one device per platform.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>NFR-15</strong></p></th><td><p>Scalability</p></td><td><p>The backend supports a growing number of families and classes without changes to the design.</p></td><td><p>Load test with simulated users.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>NFR-16</strong></p></th><td><p>Maintainability</p></td><td><p>Each AI model is a separate, versioned service that can be retrained or replaced without changing the app.</p></td><td><p>Models deployed and rolled back independently.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>NFR-17</strong></p></th><td><p>Data retention</p></td><td><p>Parents can view, export and delete their child’s data.</p></td><td><p>Export and delete work end to end.</p></td></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"panel proposal-section\" id=\"proposal-14\" aria-labelledby=\"heading-14\" hidden><h2 id=\"heading-14\">Recommended features (future work)</h2><p>These are not part of the first build. We would add them if there is time left.</p><div class=\"proposal-table table-scroll\" role=\"region\" tabindex=\"0\" aria-label=\"Recommended features (future work) — scrollable table\"><table><caption>Recommended features (future work)</caption><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\"><p><strong>FEATURE</strong></p></th><th scope=\"col\"><p><strong>WHAT IT WOULD DO</strong></p></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>Full AR companion</strong></p></th><td><p>The character sits next to the child in the real room, for example beside them at the desk during homework. It works through the phone camera, and through AR glasses if the child wears them.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>AR throughout the mobile app</strong></p></th><td><p>AR in general, not only reminders: the character appears on the table, points at things, and plays short focus games in the child’s own space.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>School learning platform integration</strong></p></th><td><p>Connects to school learning platforms, for example Google Classroom, with parent and teacher permission, so homework is imported into the AI homework assistant automatically instead of being uploaded by hand.</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p><strong>More camera-verified chores</strong></p></th><td><p>Researches further chores and verification methods, beyond examples such as cleaning the room and folding clothes, and adds them to gamified chores once they work reliably.</p></td></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"panel proposal-section\" id=\"proposal-15\" aria-labelledby=\"heading-15\" hidden><h2 id=\"heading-15\">Competitive landscape</h2><p>Several products cover a piece of what Rafeeq does. None covers the whole set. The tables compare capabilities, not quality: each product is good at what it was built for.</p><h3>Child-focused products</h3><div class=\"proposal-table table-scroll\" role=\"region\" tabindex=\"0\" aria-label=\"Child-focused products — scrollable table\"><table><caption>Child-focused products</caption><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\"></th><th scope=\"col\"><p><strong><a href=\"https://endeavorrx.com\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">EndeavorRx</a></strong></p><p>prescription game</p></th><th scope=\"col\"><p><a href=\"https://www.pearsonassessments.com/campaign/revibe.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><strong>Revibe</strong><strong> Connect</strong></a></p><p>focus watch</p></th><th scope=\"col\"><p><strong><a href=\"https://www.xr.health\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">XRHealth</a></strong></p><p>VR therapy</p></th><th scope=\"col\"><p><strong><a href=\"https://www.researchprotocols.org/2023/1/e45852\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">KeepCalm</a></strong></p><p>research, teachers</p></th><th scope=\"col\"><p><strong><a href=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10708754\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Time2Feel</a></strong></p><p>research, families</p></th><th scope=\"col\"><p><strong><a href=\"https://buddy.ai\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Buddy.ai</a></strong></p><p>kids’ learning</p></th><th scope=\"col\"><p><strong><a href=\"https://apps.apple.com/us/app/moodji-health-mood-tracker/id6470914506\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Moodji</a></strong></p><p>mood pet</p></th><th scope=\"col\"><p><strong><a href=\"https://replika.com\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Replika</a> / <a href=\"https://character.ai\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Character.AI</a></strong></p><p>AI chat</p></th><th scope=\"col\"><p><strong>Rafeeq</strong></p></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p>Built for ADHD</p></th><td><p>●</p></td><td><p>●</p></td><td><p>◐</p></td><td><p>○</p></td><td><p>○</p></td><td><p>○</p></td><td><p>○</p></td><td><p>○</p></td><td><p>●</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p>Animated conversational companion</p></th><td><p>○</p></td><td><p>○</p></td><td><p>○</p></td><td><p>○</p></td><td><p>○</p></td><td><p>●</p></td><td><p>◐</p></td><td><p>●</p></td><td><p>●</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p>Wearable sensing</p></th><td><p>○</p></td><td><p>●</p></td><td><p>○</p></td><td><p>●</p></td><td><p>●</p></td><td><p>○</p></td><td><p>●</p></td><td><p>○</p></td><td><p>●</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p>Reads emotion (voice, face, body)</p></th><td><p>○</p></td><td><p>○</p></td><td><p>○</p></td><td><p>◐</p></td><td><p>◐</p></td><td><p>○</p></td><td><p>◐</p></td><td><p>○</p></td><td><p>●</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p>Predicts focus or stress drops early</p></th><td><p>○</p></td><td><p>◐</p></td><td><p>○</p></td><td><p>○</p></td><td><p>○</p></td><td><p>○</p></td><td><p>○</p></td><td><p>○</p></td><td><p>●</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p>In-the-moment calming support</p></th><td><p>○</p></td><td><p>◐</p></td><td><p>○</p></td><td><p>●</p></td><td><p>○</p></td><td><p>○</p></td><td><p>○</p></td><td><p>◐</p></td><td><p>●</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p>Homework, task and routine support</p></th><td><p>○</p></td><td><p>◐</p></td><td><p>○</p></td><td><p>○</p></td><td><p>○</p></td><td><p>○</p></td><td><p>◐</p></td><td><p>○</p></td><td><p>●</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p>Screen-time and content intelligence</p></th><td><p>○</p></td><td><p>○</p></td><td><p>○</p></td><td><p>○</p></td><td><p>○</p></td><td><p>○</p></td><td><p>◐</p></td><td><p>○</p></td><td><p>●</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p>Parent view</p></th><td><p>●</p></td><td><p>○</p></td><td><p>○</p></td><td><p>○</p></td><td><p>●</p></td><td><p>◐</p></td><td><p>○</p></td><td><p>○</p></td><td><p>●</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p>Teacher / school view</p></th><td><p>○</p></td><td><p>○</p></td><td><p>○</p></td><td><p>●</p></td><td><p>○</p></td><td><p>◐</p></td><td><p>○</p></td><td><p>○</p></td><td><p>●</p></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\"><p>Clinician view or report</p></th><td><p>○</p></td><td><p>○</p></td><td><p>◐</p></td><td><p>○</p></td><td><p>○</p></td><td><p>○</p></td><td><p>○</p></td><td><p>○</p></td><td><p>●</p></td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>● Offered   ◐ Partly offered   ○ Not offered, or no sign of it on the product’s public page. Based on each product’s own website or paper, checked October 2026.</p><h3>What sets Rafeeq apart</h3><ul><li><strong>One system, many views:</strong> no single feature is unique. The difference is one companion with predictive sensing and role-based views (child, parent, teacher, doctor), all behind one AI system.</li><li><strong>Built around each child:</strong> the tone, animations and level of stimulation are chosen for the individual child, and he is tested in the way he answers best. We did not see this in the products above.</li><li><strong>Home, school and doctor:</strong> parents, teachers and doctors see one connected picture, instead of three separate ones.</li></ul><p>Statistics: CDC, cdc.gov/adhd/data</p></section>"};
